1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,217
Hello, Zogg here.

2
00:00:01,552 --> 00:00:04,338
Welcome to the sixth episode of Earthlings 101.

3
00:00:04,814 --> 00:00:06,474
Today we will talk about beauty.

4
00:00:42,414 --> 00:00:50,137
Beauty is a typical earthling concept: A subjective quality of objects, creatures, or even geometrical forms, or ideas.

5
00:00:50,137 --> 00:01:06,168
When you ask earthlings what beauty is, they will babble about beauty being a light in the heart, the splendor of truth, a manifestation of secret natural laws, the sensible image of the Infinite, truth's smile, eternity gazing at itself in a mirror, or even a gift from an imaginary bio administrator.

6
00:01:06,528 --> 00:01:08,657
In other words, they have no idea.

7
00:01:08,878 --> 00:01:14,538
Which is strange, as earthlings are more attracted by beauty than Centauri lawyers are attracted by a mass divorce.

8
00:01:15,538 --> 00:01:17,834
Earthlings are attracted to all kinds of things.

9
00:01:18,388 --> 00:01:23,928
Sometimes the reasons are obvious, because the things are useful for feeding, reproducing and not dying.

10
00:01:24,348 --> 00:01:31,630
But sometimes, earthlings are attracted to things that have apparently no immediate use, like flowers, music, trees or gems.

11
00:01:32,191 --> 00:01:34,572
This seemingly useless appeal is called beauty.

12
00:01:35,218 --> 00:01:37,779
An example for a beautiful object is this painting.

13
00:01:38,203 --> 00:01:42,036
For an alien, it's just a picture of a female earthling in front of a landscape.

14
00:01:42,360 --> 00:01:45,920
For earthlings, it's one of the most beautiful objects in the world.

15
00:01:46,160 --> 00:01:48,787
But what makes things like this painting so particular?

16
00:01:49,316 --> 00:02:00,036
The most obvious point of beauty is that earthlings enjoy it - and if you have learned anything during this course, it's probably that whenever earthlings enjoy something, you can be pretty sure that the genetic imperative is behind it.

17
00:02:00,376 --> 00:02:01,970
Beauty is probably no exception.

18
00:02:01,970 --> 00:02:07,419
So, instead of philosophizing about eternity and truth, let's look at some examples.

19
00:02:07,681 --> 00:02:20,881
Things earthlings usually consider beautiful include flowers, fertile landscapes, attractive but sexually unavailable females, bird songs, precious metals and gems, impressive buildings, and even interesting shapes or ideas.

20
00:02:21,461 --> 00:02:23,521
Why are earthlings attracted to those things?

21
00:02:24,481 --> 00:02:26,581
Let's pick an example: landscapes.

22
00:02:27,261 --> 00:02:34,692
Experiments have shown that earthlings consider a landscape beautiful if it is fertile, has bodies of fresh hydric acid and some trees that can be used as firewood.

23
00:02:35,175 --> 00:02:41,037
Interestingly, this is exactly the kind of landscape a bio-administrator would choose for a settlement on an alien planet.

24
00:02:41,577 --> 00:02:47,916
Usually, a bio-administrator attracts specimen to a region using a "positive psychical response field emitter", commonly known as "happybox".

25
00:02:48,570 --> 00:02:52,650
Whoever approaches the emission range of the happybox becomes instantly happy.

26
00:02:53,130 --> 00:02:59,761
So, specimen settle in these regions and start cultivating them happily, even if their efforts will only bear fruits many seasons later.

27
00:03:00,182 --> 00:03:05,319
In other words, a happybox is a bait, a substitute pleasure, a promise of future happiness.

28
00:03:05,319 --> 00:03:09,819
My guess is that beauty is exactly the same as a happybox - only without the happybox.

29
00:03:10,979 --> 00:03:16,261
Evolution favours earthlings who settle in landscapes where they can harvest food in the summer and firewood in the winter.

30
00:03:16,503 --> 00:03:20,394
But earthlings usually seek immediate pleasure rather than thinking months ahead.

31
00:03:20,840 --> 00:03:30,268
So, evolution found the trick of rewarding the discovery of fertile landscapes immediately with a substitute pleasure, some kind of virtual happy field, in order to motivate them to settle there.

32
00:03:30,608 --> 00:03:32,311
That's what earthlings call beauty.

33
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:36,040
Of course, beauty is not an actual happy field.

34
00:03:36,400 --> 00:03:45,668
It's a feeling generated by the Beast which floods the ego with pleasure when seeing a fertile landscape, or when receiving indicator signals for fertile landscapes such as flowers or bird songs.

35
00:03:46,531 --> 00:03:50,071
Now, not all beautiful things are about fertile landscapes.

36
00:03:50,331 --> 00:03:54,248
For many earthlings, "beauty" means above all "beautiful females".

37
00:03:54,537 --> 00:03:59,273
A female is considered beautiful if she is sexually attractive but not immediately sexually available.

38
00:03:59,273 --> 00:04:08,899
As I explained in episode 3, female earthlings are rather picky about their sexual partners due to their limited childbearing capacity, so males have to make an effort to get chosen.

39
00:04:09,380 --> 00:04:14,500
Now, when the female is very attractive, this courtship can last a while and involve many candidates.

40
00:04:15,180 --> 00:04:19,048
Unfortunately, earthlings prefer immediate pleasure to uncertain future delight.

41
00:04:19,048 --> 00:04:23,001
So, evolution needed a trick to motivate them not to give up too early.

42
00:04:23,406 --> 00:04:29,732
The trick was basically the same as for landscapes: Beauty, a virtual happy field around attractive but unavailable females.

43
00:04:30,318 --> 00:04:40,502
So, the thing that beautiful females and beautiful landscapes have in common is simply the fact that they have both been tagged as "beautiful" by evolution, as an indicator of fertility and a promise of future happiness.

44
00:04:41,362 --> 00:04:50,494
Males can also be beautiful, even more in a society that has started questioning its own gender roles: When males can become wooed, they need beauty to keep the wooers interested.

45
00:04:50,897 --> 00:04:54,441
But what about precious metals, gems and huge buildings?

46
00:04:54,947 --> 00:04:59,098
Well, they are indications of another thing that may lead to future happiness: Wealth.

47
00:04:59,299 --> 00:05:08,070
When an earthling, or an earthling community, is wealthy, they tend to display their wealth with rare metals, precious stones, expensive cloth and huge buildings.

48
00:05:08,070 --> 00:05:09,890
This is called pomp.

49
00:05:10,290 --> 00:05:16,332
Now, living in a wealthy city or around a wealthy person usually doesn't provide immediate pleasure, but it may pay on the long run.

50
00:05:16,593 --> 00:05:25,413
This holds for traders, mercenaries, workers, servants, prostitutes, artisans, quacks, assassins, tax investigators and many other kinds of people.

51
00:05:25,913 --> 00:05:30,026
So, human nature uses again the happy-field-trick to attract people to wealth.

52
00:05:30,453 --> 00:05:32,273
This is why pomp is considered beautiful.

53
00:05:32,913 --> 00:05:38,388
Earthlings often attach little trinkets made of precious metals and gems to their body to increase their attractivity.

54
00:05:38,611 --> 00:05:46,008
This works because being wealthy is sexy, but also because the pomp happyfield around these trinkets add to the beauty happyfield around the person.

55
00:05:46,429 --> 00:05:48,391
Earthlings call these trinkets jewelry.

56
00:05:49,126 --> 00:05:56,706
However, earthling beauty includes more than landscapes, females and pomp: Beauty can also apply to shapes, patterns or even ideas.

57
00:05:56,706 --> 00:06:04,346
This abstract beauty is determined by criteria like proportion, symmetry, smooth forms, simplicity, contrast and self-similarity.

58
00:06:05,426 --> 00:06:07,866
But why should nature tag those things as beautiful?

59
00:06:08,383 --> 00:06:12,851
Some criteria like symmetry and smooth forms may be inherited from sexual attractivity.

60
00:06:13,352 --> 00:06:17,542
Also, smooth rocks may indicate the presence of hydric acid during rainy seasons.

61
00:06:17,906 --> 00:06:21,369
But I doubt that this is enough to explain the sense of abstract beauty.

62
00:06:21,531 --> 00:06:24,388
It may have to do with solving abstract problems.

63
00:06:24,388 --> 00:06:28,472
Even complicated problems have often surprisingly simple and elegant solutions.

64
00:06:29,203 --> 00:06:34,283
Bio administrators often use this when they want to influence the thought process of some native genius creature.

65
00:06:34,667 --> 00:06:44,823
They scan the brain of the genius, observe the thought process, and then tag simple and elegant ideas with tiny happyfields, because simple and elegant ideas may lead to a simple and elegant solution.

66
00:06:45,424 --> 00:06:47,432
Abstract beauty works the same way.

67
00:06:47,763 --> 00:06:50,721
Consider a prehistoric earthling who is about to invent the wheel.

68
00:06:50,721 --> 00:06:56,746
That's what he got so far: An irregular, pentagonal wheel which works, but makes five "hops" at every turn.

69
00:06:57,146 --> 00:07:02,364
Logic may lead the earthling to think that by cutting off some corners, he can reduce the number of hops per turn.

70
00:07:02,524 --> 00:07:03,993
But that's the wrong approach.

71
00:07:04,396 --> 00:07:09,390
Now, the sense of beauty may lead him into the opposite direction, to a more elegant shape: A circle.

72
00:07:09,673 --> 00:07:13,053
And as it turns out, that's actually a pretty good shape for a wheel.

73
00:07:13,053 --> 00:07:17,134
So, when dealing with problems, beauty may be a hint to a solution.

74
00:07:17,576 --> 00:07:20,777
That's maybe why earthlings say that beauty is "the splendor of truth".

75
00:07:22,098 --> 00:07:28,098
Scientific advice: When you experiment on earthlings, you might want to examine the way their brains process patterns.

76
00:07:28,678 --> 00:07:31,640
You will notice that some patterns are easier to process than others.

77
00:07:32,232 --> 00:07:36,302
Now, there is a theory that the ease to process patterns is actually connected to - beauty!

78
00:07:37,232 --> 00:07:40,461
Earthlings call this the "processing fluency theory of aesthetic pleasure".

79
00:07:41,334 --> 00:07:42,562
Remember this painting?

80
00:07:42,823 --> 00:07:53,719
Now we understand why earthlings consider it so beautiful: It shows a wealthy, attractive, but not salacious woman in front of a rather fertile landscape, surrounded by mountains which provide fresh hydric acid.

81
00:07:54,281 --> 00:07:58,808
Besides, the painting's composition follows simple geometric rules and is almost symmetric.

82
00:07:59,229 --> 00:08:06,989
And although the painter goes easy on the pomp, the painting itself is worth roughly the equivalent of seven million Republic Credits, which certainly adds to the appeal.

83
00:08:06,989 --> 00:08:15,533
Of course, in this case, there is no real landscape, no real woman, it's all paint on sheets of cellulose, an elaborate deception of the senses.

84
00:08:16,033 --> 00:08:17,695
That's what earthlings call "art".

85
00:08:18,090 --> 00:08:20,116
We will come back to this phenomenon in a moment.

86
00:08:20,116 --> 00:08:43,851
During the last episodes, we have seen that the Beast manipulates the Ego with different kinds of pleasures, or, if you want, with different kinds of happy-fields: The fun-field around games and stories, the sexy-field around attractive earthlings, the yummy-field around food, the love-field around family, the cute-field around cubs, the funny-field which I will explain in another episode, and the beauty-field around things like flowers, jewels and hyperbolic paraboloids.

87
00:08:44,491 --> 00:08:51,156
As beauty is kind of an all-purpose happyfield, nature uses it often to amplify sexy-fields, cute-fields and love-fields.

88
00:08:52,260 --> 00:08:54,918
So, earthlings may find these things beautiful.

89
00:08:54,918 --> 00:09:14,029
Now, earthlings have learned to imitate the stimuli that generate all these happyfields: Some drugs imitate fun, porn imitates sexual stimuli, diet drinks imitate sweetness, stuffed animals and dolls imitate loved beings, lolcats imitate small animals, jokes imitate funny situations, and art imitates the stimuli of beauty.

90
00:09:14,029 --> 00:09:19,510
The phenomenon of art is similar to what happens to some administrated planets when the bio-administrator is gone.

91
00:09:20,114 --> 00:09:24,933
Sometimes, the natives find a remaining happybox, retro-engineer it and build their own happyboxes.

92
00:09:26,118 --> 00:09:32,659
These boxes are then sold, put onto public places and into showrooms, where they flood the environment with totally pointless happywaves.

93
00:09:33,439 --> 00:09:39,865
Rich natives wear tiny happy-boxes around their neck to be more appealing, or exhibit the boxes in their house to show their wealth.

94
00:09:40,469 --> 00:09:50,189
Individuals who conceive new happywave patterns become rich and famous, companies use happy-boxes for advertizing, and the production of boxes and happywave patterns becomes a whole new industry.

95
00:09:51,129 --> 00:09:53,771
Exactly the same happens on earth - only without the happyboxes.

96
00:09:55,223 --> 00:10:01,004
Earthlings called artists have learned to imitate the stimuli of beauty in paintings, sculptures, and so on.

97
00:10:01,387 --> 00:10:03,252
These objects are called artworks.

98
00:10:03,576 --> 00:10:13,618
Art is related to natural beauty like diet drinks are related to actual sugar, or porn to actual sex: They imitate the stimuli of pleasure, but out of context and deprived of their utility.

99
00:10:13,618 --> 00:10:23,418
Not surprisingly, earthling art often focuses on beautiful people, and on the abundance of nature - landscapes, flowers, fruits, and all kinds of living creatures.

100
00:10:23,798 --> 00:10:25,718
Except for the microbes.

101
00:10:29,018 --> 00:10:30,258
Tips for tourists.

102
00:10:30,738 --> 00:10:33,578
If you are interested in art, visit an art museum.

103
00:10:33,958 --> 00:10:39,036
However, you should avoid modern art, as it often challenges or distorts the concept of beauty.

104
00:10:39,379 --> 00:10:42,559
Better start with classical art, it's much easier to understand.

105
00:10:43,539 --> 00:10:51,459
Art is not limited to visual stimuli: One of the most important indicators of fertile regions are bird songs, which are in consequence considered beautiful.

106
00:10:51,999 --> 00:10:57,037
Now, earthlings have developed something that sounds like an attempt to imitate bird songs: Music.

107
00:10:57,438 --> 00:11:04,147
The most common methods to make music are singing, blowing into metal tubes, and using vibrating strings attached to hollow boxes.

108
00:11:04,687 --> 00:11:11,987
The development of music has probably also been influenced by prehistoric rituals, sexual selection, and by imitation of bird songs for hunting.

109
00:11:12,707 --> 00:11:20,002
Earthlings love beauty so much that they even conceive everyday objects to be all artsy and beautiful, like clothing, chairs or buildings.

110
00:11:21,004 --> 00:11:22,168
Earthlings call this "design".

111
00:11:22,687 --> 00:11:28,160
Like art, design follows trends - what is beautiful today may become ugly in some decades.

112
00:11:28,160 --> 00:11:32,960
A particularly interesting case of design are individual vehicles called cars.

113
00:11:33,340 --> 00:11:37,347
Regardless of trends, cars have always a stylized face on the front.

114
00:11:37,748 --> 00:11:38,948
Why is this so?

115
00:11:39,228 --> 00:11:40,172
I've no idea.

116
00:11:40,694 --> 00:11:49,532
Maybe earthlings are afraid of fast moving faceless objects, like landslides, forest fires, killer waves, or swarms of furious insects.

117
00:11:49,973 --> 00:11:52,853
So they put faces onto their cars to make them less frightening.

118
00:11:55,033 --> 00:11:55,906
Strategic advice.

119
00:11:56,398 --> 00:12:01,837
If you want to gain the earthlings' trust before taking over the planet, it won't be easy if they think your ships are ugly.

120
00:12:02,142 --> 00:12:06,554
So you might want to abduct some earthling designers and have them design a beautiful landing shuttle.

121
00:12:07,069 --> 00:12:13,002
Elegant forms, white paint and a touch of blue or green are great for convincing earthlings that you "come in peace".

122
00:12:13,471 --> 00:12:22,891
However, you should go easy on the pomp because A), earthlings associate exaggerated pomp with tyrants and crime lords, and B), the natives may be tempted to steal your spaceship.

123
00:12:27,111 --> 00:12:31,002
Art may seem like an absurdity, a self-deception, a global joke.

124
00:12:31,285 --> 00:12:36,355
But you can also see it from another angle: As an attempt of mankind to grow beyond the necessity of biology.

125
00:12:36,938 --> 00:12:49,888
Earthlings live under the tyranny of the genetic imperative, much more than they generally realize - but maybe art, and beauty in general, are a way of escaping from the grip of this tyrant, of tricking their own nature into giving them happiness without biological necessity.

126
00:12:49,888 --> 00:12:54,941
On the other hand, you could say the same about lolcats, diet drinks or porn.

127
00:12:55,161 --> 00:12:55,681
So...

128
00:12:56,241 --> 00:12:59,163
Anyway, this was episode 6 of Earthlings 101.

129
00:12:59,828 --> 00:13:04,848
On a personal note, I will be on vacation next month; I plan to visit the orbital castles of erioL.

130
00:13:05,588 --> 00:13:12,734
So, the next episode will probably not come before the Galactic Nerdfighter Day, or, in earthling terms, beginning of September.

131
00:13:13,075 --> 00:13:19,260
In the next episode, we will learn about the social mechanisms behind words like "hello", "please", "thank you" and "sorry".

132
00:13:19,260 --> 00:13:22,812
We also learn why international karma trade is big business.

133
00:13:23,256 --> 00:13:25,166
As always, thanks for watching.

134
00:13:25,491 --> 00:13:27,194
And don't forget to be alien!

